\description{
Searches across multiple component pairs for the closest decision boundary
to the target observation in the rotated biplot space. For each pair, the
loading matrix is rotated so that the target observation is aligned with the
plane of that pair (see \strong{Rotation}), then a fine grid is scored and
the boundary contour is extracted.
}
\section{Rotation}{

The rotation adapts \code{Biplot_rotation()} from the research script
(\file{scripts/1.3 Optimal Rotation.R} on the \code{original_PhD_code}
branch of the repository). An SVD-based orthogonal rotation
matrix \eqn{A} is computed such that the rotated loading matrix aligns the
target observation with the projection plane defined by \code{proj_pair}.
This gives each component pair the best possible chance of revealing a
nearby boundary. Each pair of components labels one plane through the
target point; since the Procrustes rotation turns the plane, the selected
plane need not coincide with the plane spanned by the two components that
name it.
}

\section{Constraints}{

The convex hull polygon of the global biplot (from \code{bl_build_grid()})
is \strong{not} used here, because the per-pair rotation invalidates its
coordinates. Three constraint types are applied instead, in this order:
\describe{
\item{hull}{When \code{hull = TRUE} (the default), a boundary vertex must
lie inside the convex hull of the training data and the target,
projected onto the rotated plane of that pair. The hull is rebuilt for
every pair, as in Appendix A of the thesis, and applies its
plausibility rule (section 4.3.1): the counterfactual is selected from
a local decision boundary within the convex hull of the training data.
\code{hull = FALSE} drops this rule.}
\item{set_filters}{Actionability constraints supplied via
\code{\link{set_filters}}.}
\item{train_ranges}{Back-projected X-space values must lie within the
per-variable ranges observed in the training data.}
}
The hull applies to the boundary points on the plane. The restored
(\code{"fixed"}) and rounded (\code{"integer"}) points lie off the plane,
and are checked against the constraints and \code{train_ranges} only.
}

\section{Fixed constraints}{

During the search a \code{"fixed"} feature may move by up to 0.5 in model
units, because a vertex of the 2D boundary contour rarely holds a feature at
exactly its observed value. After the best pair is chosen, each fixed
feature the search moved is set back to its observed value and the point is
re-scored. If the restored point still crosses the decision boundary and
lies within \code{train_ranges}, it becomes the counterfactual
(\code{B_x}, \code{B_z}, \code{B_pred}). Otherwise no counterfactual holds
the fixed features, and none is reported: \code{solution_found} is
\code{FALSE}, and \code{blocking_constraint} gives the fixed values the
search reached, why the restored point fails, and that changing the
constraints on the actionable features may make a counterfactual possible.
Either way \code{fixed_check} records what happened. The restored point
satisfies the fixed constraint exactly but lies off the 2D boundary, so it
need not be the nearest valid point; the pair choice and the reported
distances are those of the search.
}

\section{Integer features}{

A feature marked \code{"integer"} in \code{\link{set_filters}} is searched
as a continuous value, and rounded after the fixed restore. For each such
feature whose counterfactual value is not a whole number in raw units, the
whole numbers just below and just above are tried, and every combination
of them is a candidate. A candidate is valid when the model still scores
it on the other side of the cutoff, it meets every \code{set_filters()}
constraint and it lies within \code{train_ranges}. The valid candidate
nearest the target in X-space, by the same distance as the pair search
(Mahalanobis, or Euclidean on the total-SD scale), is the
counterfactual (\code{B_x}, \code{B_z}, \code{B_pred}); on a tie, the
first. When no candidate is valid, no counterfactual is reported:
\code{solution_found} is \code{FALSE}, and \code{blocking_constraint} says
why and that changing the other constraints, or removing \code{"integer"},
may make one possible. \code{integer_check} records what happened. Like a
restored point, the rounded point lies off the 2D boundary; the reported
distances are those of the search. Up to 12 integer features can be
rounded together (\eqn{2^{12}} candidates).
}

\section{Cross-pair selection criterion}{

The best component pair is selected by minimum distance from the target
to the nearest valid boundary vertex, measured in X-space with
\eqn{v = B_x - x_{obs}}. Two distance metrics are available:
\describe{
\item{\code{"mahalanobis"}}{Squared Mahalanobis distance using the
within-class covariance matrix \eqn{W} stored on
\code{bl_result$metric_inv}: \eqn{d_M^2 = v^T W^{-1} v}.}
\item{\code{"euclidean"}}{Euclidean distance with each feature divided by
its total standard deviation (\code{bl_result$X_sd}):
\eqn{d_x = \sqrt{\sum_j (v_j / sd_j)^2}}, as \code{dist_x} in
\code{\link{bl_find_boundary}}.}
}
The default, \code{"auto"}, follows the thesis rule (section 3.9.1):
Euclidean distance for a PCA biplot and Mahalanobis distance for a CVA
biplot. Under a standardised PCA the rotated loadings are orthonormal, so
for the search's point on the plane \eqn{d_x} equals the 2D distance
\eqn{d_z = ||Z_{target} - B_z||_2}. Under CVA the loadings satisfy
\eqn{V^T W V = I / (n - g)}, every rotated basis inherits this, and so
\eqn{d_M^2 = (n - g) d_z^2} for every pair: the Mahalanobis rule ranks
the pairs as the 2D distance does. The two metrics measure the same way
after the \code{"fixed"} restore and the \code{"integer"} rounding move the
point off the plane. Within-pair selection (the nearest valid contour
vertex) always uses 2D Euclidean distance in the rotated Z-space; only the
cross-pair comparison and the integer rounding use \code{distance}. See
\code{documentation/biplot-rotation/biplot_rotation_and_pair_selection.md}
section 3.4 in the package repository.
}

